/// An integer may be written in base 16, 8 or 2.
///
/// 0xFFFF, 0o1234 and 0b1111 all scan today as the digit 0 followed by an
/// identifier -- xFFFF, o1234, b1111 -- so a program using one fails in the
/// parser with a message about the surrounding expression rather than about the
/// literal.
///
/// ⚠️ A leading zero does NOT make a literal octal, and this test pins that.
/// 0123 is one hundred and twenty-three.  C's rule has cost the world more
/// debugging hours than it ever saved keystrokes, and the 0o prefix exists so
/// the language never needs it.
///
/// Every form denotes a VALUE rather than a bit pattern, which is why 0xFFFFFFFF
/// is 4294967295 and not -1.  That follows from the literal rule -- narrowest of
/// Integer and Long that holds the value -- applying to every base alike, and it
/// is where the language parts company with Java.
///
/// Fails with: Expect ')' after arguments.  (file rejected -- no test ran)
///
test 'An integer may be written in hex, octal or binary';
begin
    AssertEqual (65535, 0xFFFF);
    AssertEqual (65535, 0XFFFF);
    AssertEqual (65535, 0xffff);
    AssertEqual (668,   0o1234);
    AssertEqual (15,    0b1111);
    AssertEqual (123,   0123);
end
